var e=`# CHAPTER 6 — The Primordial

They didn't stay only on his back.

Kim closed his fist and punched the arena floor.

The impact sounded like thunder under the earth. The stone shattered, opened a crooked crater and spat rock shards everywhere. He used the chaos itself to throw himself upward and landed on his feet, on guard.

The arena went silent.

Then came the murmur. The whole stands, low, like a wave coming in.

— He changed his avatar?! — A masked spectator, on his feet.

Bug. White hair. What the hell. The whole chat asking the same thing.

---

Thunderbolt felt his blood run cold.

Kim's hair was white. A silver white that floated around his face like threads of liquid silver. His chest bare, the muscles in a way a human body doesn't get. And around him, dancing, black flames like the night. Hissing. Sinuous. Like a venomous snake, its dark tongue licking the air.

The flames seemed to sniff Thunderbolt.

It wasn't ordinary fear. It was older. It was what prey feels when it understands there's a predator in front of it. He tried to step back and his body moved slow, as if walking through molasses.

— What the fuck kind of skin is this? — Thunderbolt, between his teeth.

He swallowed dry.

— That's not a man.

Kim set his knee on the ground.

He stayed there a moment. The whole arena holding its breath.

And he drove his foot down against the soil.

The fissures ran across the floor in every direction, like lightning. A cloud of golden dust rose and swallowed Kim.

When the dust settled, he was standing. Arms open. Hands curved into claws, fingers hard as blades. And his eyes.

The iris was gone. In its place, two small black fires, alive.

The crowd exploded. Shouting, applause, people on their feet. Everyone feeling they were watching a legend being born.

---

In the stands, Jack watched with fascination and horror on the same pale face.

— Kim...? — Almost voiceless. — What the fuck is this, man?!

The friend he knew had disappeared. In his place was a wild thing, moving with the fluidity of a born predator.

---

Kim lowered his palms to the ground.

On all fours. Fingers curved, nails scraping the earth. His breathing deep, controlled. His lips opened and showed clenched teeth.

It was the smile of someone who had already chosen his prey.

— Where are you going, prey?

And he exploded.

A blur. Thunderbolt threw a punch by instinct. Hit air.

— Where did he go?!

— Here.

Crouched beneath him.

One hand on the back of Thunderbolt's neck. The other on his temple. An almost gentle touch. And the giant couldn't move, like a marionette whose strings someone had found.

The knee came up.

Bone against bone. The sound ran through the arena like dry thunder. Thunderbolt's jaw shattered.

A heartbeat of silence.

The giant collapsed on his back. The ground shook with the weight of the armor.

— BRUUUTAAAL! — The Announcer, from above.

Then the whole arena screamed along.

— He's destroying the guy! — Jack, gripping the rail.

Kim looked at the giant on the ground. Tilted his head.

— It's not over yet.

---

Thunderbolt got up spitting blood. Twelve hundred fights didn't let him stay down.

He opened his metal hand. The palm lit up.

*CELESTIAL LIGHTNING.*

— You miserable inferior... die!

The bolt crossed the arena and hit Kim in the chest.

The black aura swallowed it. Just like that. Like an animal swallowing food.

Kim laughed.

— Was that an attack or a caress?

— H-how...?!

---

The giant opened both hands. Planted his feet. Rotated his hips and let everything go at once.

*CELESTIAL LIGHTNING x4.*

— Fall! Fall! Fall!

Four bolts. Four thunderclaps in sequence.

The flames around Kim turned into serpent heads. Four open mouths. Each one caught a bolt in midair and chewed.

Nom. Nom.

Thunderbolt was panting, his shoulders rising and falling.

— That it? — Kim. — I thought it was going to be fun.

---

The spear came.

*CELESTIAL THRUST.*

— Hold still, you bastard!

Thrust. Thrust. Thrust. The full weight of his body behind each one.

Kim just moved his torso out of the way. Left. Right. He left an afterimage in the air.

— Left... right... you're slow, old man.

On the last one, he caught the spear tip with his bare hand.

He sniffed the air.

— Hmm. Smell of fear.

---

The giant pulled the spear back and spun his whole body in a low arc. To take out the legs.

*ARC SWEEP.*

— Fall already!

Kim crouched flush with the earth. The blade passed over his head whistling.

— Now!

The punch came next. And another. And another.

Nothing. Kim left a blue trail across the arena and always appeared somewhere else.

He appeared behind him. Calm.

— You lost? I'm right here.

— How are you seeing this?!

— Seeing? I'm just walking.

---

Thunderbolt drove the spear into the ground.

*LIVING GROUND.*

— Fry, you bastard!

The electricity ran through the whole arena. The floor became a blue crackling carpet.

Kim looked down, annoyed.

— Too much light. I'll put it out.

The aura poured from his body onto the floor and ate the light. Piece by piece. Until only dark remained.

— There. Now it's cozy.

---

Across the way, Jack saw every detail.

The tribal tattoos climbing Kim's neck, alive. Black lines pulsing and spreading like ink veins, descending from his shoulders. His hair turning silver strand by strand.

But it was the face.

Jack knew the language of violence. He had read bloodlust in the eyes of opponents who wouldn't quit. He had seen pure fury.

Never, ever had he seen that hunger on his friend's face.

It was as if an ancient beast had woken behind Kim's eyes.

---

Kim closed his hand around Thunderbolt's neck. And lifted the giant off the ground like picking up a leaf.

With the tip of his index finger, he touched the destroyed jaw. Almost tenderly. The blood stuck to his finger.

— It's been so long... — The voice was Kim's. But it carried something else underneath. — Since I felt the warmth of human blood.

Thunderbolt tried to speak. From his shattered jaw, only a throat sound came out.

Kim brought his finger to his mouth. He licked the blood slowly, smiling.

— And how simple it is to take it from you mortals.

And he rose.

From the soles of his feet, black flames began to climb. Coiling around his body like an entity. The sound of the fire was sharp, cutting. And anyone with good ears could hear, amid the crackling, something else.

Screaming. Wailing. Whispers of suffering that never ended.

— The Primordial Blood is too powerful. — His eyes swept the arena, now with an amber gleam. — Almost no vessel can withstand it.

---

He dropped Thunderbolt on the ground. Raised his right hand to the sky.

— Let's see how much this body can take.

— Kim...? — Jack, from the stands.

A silence before the tragedy.

Then he roared.

A roar that came from the depths of something far older than him. The shockwave left his body in visible circles, tearing through the air.

The arena, built for the heaviest fights, groaned. The marble pillars burst like glass. The stands, packed a second before, were swept away like dry leaves. Stone and metal flying everywhere.

Jack threw himself behind a wall.

— Holy shit!

The SBI cameras shook and lost signal.

Millions of people watching on PowWow were left staring at a static screen.

*Where's the live? Come back! Did it drop?*

---

When the broadcast returned, the dust was settling.

And in the middle of the ruins was something else.

His hair was now made of white filament, like fiber optic, smooth to his shoulders, each strand pulsing with a black-electric glow. The tattoos had become deep ritual channels taking over his whole body, climbing his neck in geometry and descending his arms like a river. The Xikaxi symbols, wilder, pulsing under the skin.

A black leather coat to his knees, cut crooked, open at the chest, covered in crow feathers, animal bones and claws. Leather pants with bone plates on the sides, symbols burned into them. Boots that seemed to swallow light, crossed by blue veins.

Living black war paint on his face. His eyes, two white spheres without irises. Pieces of bone piercing his skin in some spots, as if they had grown from the inside out. A necklace of bone, teeth and claws, and each piece held a red flame. In place of a headdress, filaments of blue energy rising from his head, waving, vanishing and returning. Green veins pulsing around his eyes. And two red lightning-shaped marks cutting across his cheeks.

Primordial Kim.

— Finally...

---

Thunderbolt was still alive.

On his knees in the middle of the wreckage, armor cracked, he raised his spear to the sky. Above the arena, a sphere of electricity the size of a building began to spin.

*LAST RESORT — THRONE OF THUNDER.*

— I was saving this for the top of the ranking... — The voice came out ragged from his broken jaw. — But you're going to disappear now!

The sphere descended.

The impact erased the center of the arena in a white flash. The ground sank. Thunderbolt fell to his knees, bleeding, breathless.

— Hah... hah... turned to dust.

The dust settled.

Primordial Kim was in the same spot. Yawning.

— Haaam...

He brushed the dust off his shoulder with two fingers.

— All right. Now it's my turn. — He closed his fists. — I'll just throw a few little punches.

You couldn't see it. Only hear it.

One crack linked to the next, so fast it became a single sound. Thunderbolt's armor denting in a hundred places at once. His body shaking in the air, pinned by the blows, unable to fall.

*50000 HIT COMBO.*

— BRUUUTAAAL! — The Announcer, both hands on his head.

Kim stopped. Thunderbolt hung from his fist, bloodied, without strength even to groan.

Primordial Kim looked at his own hands. The lines glowing on his skin.

— That's better.

---

*Is it still Brawler 142536?*

The question ran through the chat of the whole world. *Avatar change is a ban!* The rule was clear: changing avatars mid-match is forbidden. Immediate ban. And there was proof that it had happened.

Some moved away from the screen, as if the danger could cross over. Others stayed frozen. And a few had a strange, focused expression, as if a very old memory was trying to surface.

Outside the game, hidden in the shadow of the annex, a girl with pink hair watched on the screen. Her face still. Her eye narrowed.

— I've seen this before... — Nina, low. — Where?

---

In the arena, Primordial Kim looked at his own body from head to toe.

— What ridiculous garments. — He tugged at the coat with contempt. — They must be the controls of this simulation.

He turned his eye back to Thunderbolt, still hanging from his fist.

— I'll worry about that later. It's time for my millennia-old meal.

He raised his arm. A muffled explosion, and black flames sprouted from his palm like a poisonous flower opening.

Thunderbolt tried to pull back. He babbled. Shook his head. No, no, no.

---

A few meters away, Jack watched it with horror.

Since the match had taken that turn, a dread had lodged in his chest. Now he was certain.

— That's not Kim.

---

— These small creatures have a hunger that is never sated. — Primordial Kim, the flames dancing between his fingers. — You barely serve as an appetizer.

He laughed. A guttural, primitive laugh.

His fingers closed around Thunderbolt's neck. The pressure rose slowly. Methodical. Air became a luxury. Thunderbolt grabbed his fist with both hands, and every attempt to breathe came out as a wheeze.

— The agony you'll feel will cross the border between this plane and reality. It will live in every fiber of you. And I guarantee: if you survive, you will never forget what happened today.

Pause.

— Consider yourself privileged. Few people have the honor of feeding such... special creatures.

---

He released the flames.

They poured to the ground like living liquid. They weren't fire. They were animal. A predator waking from a thousand-year sleep.

And they started at the feet.

Slowly. Savoring.

Thunderbolt's scream tore through the arena. It wasn't just pain. It was the despair of someone feeling their own existence being eaten piece by piece.

The flames didn't burn. They devoured. The skin didn't char. It vanished, absorbed, as if it had never existed. He felt his bones turning to a powder the wind wouldn't even bother to carry.

Until there was nothing left.

Not ash. Not darkness. An absence so deep that the space seemed to have forgotten someone had existed there.

The flames returned to Primordial Kim's hands like a tame animal returning to its owner.

— Darkness is the beginning and the end of everything. — To the void. — Never forget that, little meal.

He tilted his head. Scratched his temple with his finger, distracted. Put his hands on his hips and looked at the ground, searching for crumbs.

— Truly... that was disappointing.

---

The arena didn't know what to do.

A bronze-muscled warrior frowning. A hooded mage whispering. A winged creature hovering restless in the air.

A gearwork automaton was first. Raised its metal arms and cheered.

Then it burst. Some screaming, some open-mouthed, still trying to understand.

And in the middle of it all, alone, Jack was applauding.

Clapping in rhythm, a proud smile on his face. He had bet all his chips on his friend.

— I won the bet!

---

The Announcer came gliding across the arena. An elegant and grotesque figure, limbs too long, moving like a spider. He grabbed Primordial Kim's wrist and raised it to the sky.

— Beings and beings! We have witnessed something extraordinary! He whom we judged to be merely an ordinary boy has revealed himself as an undeniable primordial force! When all seemed lost, he emerged as the fearsome Snowy Heartthrob, dominating the battlefield! — He extended the microphone. — And now, great champion, how do you wish to be hailed?

Primordial Kim looked at the microphone with a very ancient curiosity.

— I am The Primordial.

As if it were the most obvious thing in the world.

— The Primordial! — The Announcer, in ecstasy. — The Primordial! The Primordial!

The whole arena chanted along.

---

In the middle of the glory, something went wrong.

A spasm crossed Primordial Kim's body. His hands contracted on their own. The glow in his eyes flickered, like a flame in the wind.

— No... no, no, no!

The voice that was power now was despair.

— That weak worm... — Teeth clenched. — Only gave me a few minutes! I will return!

The return was pain. Muscles shrinking. Hair darkening, losing its shine, shortening. Skin returning to the tone of any teenager.

He fell to his knees with his hands on his chest. It was as if every cell was being rewritten.

The crowd went quiet.

The body collapsed on the arena floor.

Nobody moved.

— Did he... die? — Someone in the stands.

Jack was already vaulting the rail. Running through the wreckage.

— Kim!

A few seconds of just ragged breathing.

Then a much smaller figure began to stir in the middle of the rubble.

---

Kim opened his eyes.

The usual Kim. Human. His pupils lost, like someone waking from a deep, confused dream. He got up staggering, legs shaking. The blue cap had fallen off at some point. The dark T-shirt was torn.

He looked at his own hands. Small. Ordinary. Then at the crowd staring at him.

— What... what's happening?

Almost soundless.

Numbers and data dancing at the edge of his vision. The cries of "The Primordial" still echoing in his head. Everything a blur of color and noise that his brain couldn't organize.

*SCORE +9999. SYNC 47%. ERROR.*

The arena began to dissolve into cubes. His body too.

---

*Your match has just ended. Your score has been updated. You may remove the SBI safely.*

NeoGuide's voice, from far away.

— Wait...!

The school annex came back all at once.

Cold concrete under his knees. The wind through the glassless window. The smell of mildew and old cigarettes.

Kim pulled the SBI off his head. His legs wobbled like bamboo. His body was drained in a way that had nothing to do with LDI damage. It was another kind of exhaustion. As if something had used his body as fuel without asking permission.

He searched for Jack with his eyes.

And what he saw left him floored.

Jack was on the ground. White as paper. Scrambling backward on his ass, hands raised in front of his body.

— S-stay there... don't come near...

— Jack... what is it?

— Who... who are you?

In the headset lying on the floor, for a second, Kim's reflection shone with different eyes.

*...I will return.*

Kim took a step.

— Calm down, Jack! It's me!

— GET OUT OF ME, SATAN!

Kim stopped. He looked at his own hands.

— What... what's happening to me? — Panting. — I'm... so weak...

---

Then he remembered the bet.

Even like that, the first thing his eye searched for was the money.

And his eye found Freddy.

On the ground, three meters away. Out cold. The SBI still on his face. As still as Thunderbolt had been in the arena.

A coldness climbed Kim's spine.

*Did I...*

He stared at the boy's chest. One second. Two. An eternity.

The chest rose.

Fell.

The air left Kim all at once. He looked for a place to sit before his legs decided for him, found a piece of wall and slid down to the floor.

On the other side of the annex, Jack hadn't moved. He kept looking at Kim. Cautious. Like someone looking at an animal that just woke up.

— Kim. — Low. — Is it really you in there?

Kim pulled a crumpled cigarette from his pocket. His fingers trembled a little.

— Who else would it be, Jack? Holy shit.

— I don't know. Maybe the fucking demon that was possessing your body inside the game.

Kim let out an awkward laugh. He wiped his forehead.

Since the light had exploded in front of his eyes, it was as if a curtain had dropped over everything. Loose pieces danced at the edge of his mind. Distorted sound. The sensation of not being in control of his own body.

Nothing you could call a memory.

*...The Pri... mor... dial...*

— I don't remember anything.

---

The SBI lying on the floor, the old one Freddy had lent, beeped.

A rhythmic beeping, almost a song. The blue light pulsing on the annex walls.

Jack got up slowly. He looked at the device as if it might tell him something he wasn't sure he wanted to know.

— NeoGuide. Display the message.

*Position update in the Dynamic Ranking System. Congratulations, Brawler 142536. Your current position is 1,201.*

Jack pulled his own SBI from his pocket, checked the screen and laughed to himself.

— Jack Cachorrão 987654321. Position 10,776. So long grinding in this shit, and you pass me in one fight.

Kim and Jack looked at each other.

Kim, lost. Eyebrows drawn together, searching for an answer in his friend's face.

Jack, holding back a smile that didn't fit his mouth.

*New features unlocked: nickname customization, access to advanced training area, and activation of the monetization system.*

— Fuck! — Jack exploded. — We made it! Fucking awesome, Kim! Fucking awesome!

— Dude. Stop with the damn show and explain what the fuck is going on. — Kim. — Start by telling me how I turned that fight around.

Jack opened his mouth to answer.

And closed it. There were footsteps in the annex.

---

Light steps, over the rubble. Calculated.

A girl stepped out of the shadow. On the way, she stepped on one of the unconscious goons on the floor like stepping on a rug. She walked loose, almost feline.

Pink hair.

Nina.

She looked at the scene. At Freddy out cold. At the five goons still lying where they'd fallen. At Kim sitting against the wall with an unlit cigarette between his fingers.

— Good fight. — With a face of rehearsed boredom. — Congratulations.

She came closer, measured steps.

— Give me a cigarette?

Pause.

— You're Kim, right? Or do you prefer I call you The Primordial?

Kim and Jack looked at each other. The same question on both: how the hell did this girl get here?

Nina drummed her fingers on her thigh, impatient.

— If you're wondering how I got here, it's simple. I followed you two. I've been watching since the first punch. — She tilted her head. — So? Are you going to offer me a cigarette or am I going to have to beg?
`;export{e as default};
//# sourceMappingURL=capitulo-06-DkJk55py.js.map